{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011Cg1dz871wpTZH7qyAY4M8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The memory directory is blocked by the sandbox in this session, so I'll skip saving project context and go straight to the deliberation.\n\n## The council\n\n**The accountant.** You're asking the wrong question. A ledger is append-only by definition, and that predates event sourcing by five centuries. You never UPDATE a ledger row. A mistake is fixed by posting a reversing entry, not by editing the original. \"CRUD with an audit trigger\" on a ledger means you're planning to mutate balances and hope a trigger remembers what happened. Auditors will not accept that, and neither should you. But I'm not voting for event sourcing either. Double-entry journal lines are facts, not commands you replay. Nobody needs projections to compute a balance. A SUM does it.\n\n**The shipping lead.** Six weeks, four people, production. Full event sourcing means event schema versioning, upcasters, snapshotting, projection rebuilds, and a read model that lags writes. That's two of your six weeks spent on plumbing nobody will thank you for, and the one person who understands it becomes a single point of failure. I have watched this exact team shape ship an event store and then spend the next year explaining to support why the dashboard shows a number the invoice doesn't. CRUD plus a generic jsonb audit trigger is a one-day job in Postgres. Ship it.\n\n**The event-sourcing advocate.** The audit trigger records that a row changed. It does not record why. When finance asks \"what did Acme owe on March 3rd under the tax rules in force then,\" you'll be reconstructing it from row diffs with no business meaning. When a pricing bug corrupts a thousand invoices, with events you re-run the rating logic. With CRUD you write a one-off script and pray. The shipping lead is right that the framework tax is real. But intent capture is the thing you cannot bolt on later, because the data is already gone.\n\n**The premise skeptic.** Two claims in the question are doing a lot of work and both are shaky. First, \"migrating off means rewriting the billing core.\" That's only true if the storage model leaks into every caller. Put the ledger behind four functions, post, reverse, balance, and list entries, and the storage underneath is swappable. The rewrite is a design choice you haven't made yet. Second, the asymmetry: going from append-only to full event sourcing is easy, because your entries already are the events. Going from mutable rows to anything is hard, because you've lost the facts. So the real one-way door is mutability, not the ES framework.\n\n**The ops and data person.** Whatever you pick, finance will want to open it in Metabase. Event streams aren't queryable by a non-engineer. Mutable CRUD tables are, but they're wrong by the accountant's argument. An append-only entries table with a materialized balance table is both queryable and correct. Also, nobody mentioned that audit triggers are bypassable by anyone with superuser, so if the trigger is your only integrity story, your integrity story is \"we trust everyone with prod access.\" Revoke UPDATE and DELETE on the ledger at the role level and let the trigger be belt-and-braces.\n\n## The verdict\n\nBuild an append-only double-entry ledger table, and plain CRUD with an audit trigger for everything that isn't the ledger. Do not build event sourcing.\n\nThis is not a compromise. The accountant, the skeptic, and the ops person converge on it for different reasons. The shipping lead gets a design that's a few days of work, not weeks. The ES advocate gets intent capture, because every ledger entry carries a typed reason and a reference to what caused it. The one dissent that stands: if you ever need to re-rate historical usage under new rules, you'll want the raw usage events too. That's a metering concern, not an invoicing one, and the skeptic's point holds. Store raw usage append-only from day one if you have it, and you've kept that door open at near-zero cost.\n\n**What to build**\n\n- One `ledger_entries` table. Each business transaction posts a balanced set of debit and credit lines in one database transaction. Enforce balance with a deferred constraint trigger.\n- No UPDATE or DELETE, ever. Revoke both at the role level for the app user, plus a trigger that raises on either. Corrections are reversing entries.\n- Every entry carries an entry type, a source reference such as the invoice or payment id, an idempotency key with a unique index, an actor, and a timestamp. That's your \"why.\"\n- Amounts as integer minor units plus a currency code. Never a float, never a numeric you round later.\n- Balances as a materialized table updated in the same transaction as the posting. Not eventually consistent. SUM over entries is the correctness check, run nightly.\n- Invoices, customers, plans, and the rest are ordinary mutable tables with a generic jsonb audit trigger. Set the acting user per transaction with a session variable so the trigger captures who, not just what.\n- Expose the ledger through a thin posting module. Nothing else touches the table directly. This is what makes the \"rewrite\" claim false.\n\n**When to overrule this**\n\nPick real event sourcing only if the core product is usage-based rating where rules change retroactively and you must recompute past periods. For discrete B2B invoices with line items, that's not you, and the council was unanimous on that point even while disagreeing on everything else."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2493,"cache_read_input_tokens":16203,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2493},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},